# TypeScript Conventions

## Import Organization
Always organize imports in this order:
1. React and Next.js imports
2. Third-party libraries
3. Internal components and utilities
4. Type-only imports (use `import type`)

```typescript
"use client";

import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button";
import { Badge } from "@/components/ui/badge";
import Link from "next/link";
import { motion } from "framer-motion";
import type { Metadata } from "next";
```

## Component Patterns

### Interface Definitions
- Define interfaces for component props at the top of the file
- Use descriptive names ending with `Props`
- Make optional props explicit with `?`

```typescript
interface HeroProps {
  badgeText: string;
  title: string;
  description: string;
  primaryButton?: {
    text: string;
    href: string;
  };
  secondaryButton?: {
    text: string;
    href: string;
  };
}
```

### Component Exports
- Use named exports for components
- Export interfaces when they might be reused
- Use default exports only for page components

```typescript
export function Hero({ badgeText, title, description }: HeroProps) {
  // component implementation
}
```

## Type Safety

### Strict TypeScript Configuration
The project uses strict TypeScript settings from [tsconfig.json](mdc:tsconfig.json):
- `strict: true`
- `noEmit: true`
- `isolatedModules: true`

### Centralized Type System
Use the centralized type system from [src/lib/types.ts](mdc:src/lib/types.ts):
```typescript
import type { BaseComponentProps, ButtonAction, ErrorContext } from '@/lib/types';

interface MyComponentProps extends BaseComponentProps {
  title: string;
  action?: ButtonAction;
}
```

### Type Utilities
Use advanced type utilities from [src/lib/type-utils.ts](mdc:src/lib/type-utils.ts):
```typescript
import type { FormErrors, ApiRequestParams } from '@/lib/type-utils';

type MyFormErrors = FormErrors<MyFormData>;
type MyApiParams = ApiRequestParams<MyApiData>;
```

### Path Aliases
Always use path aliases for imports:
- `@/components/*` for components
- `@/lib/*` for utilities
- `@/hooks/*` for custom hooks

### Form Validation
Use Zod for form validation with React Hook Form:
```typescript
import { z } from "zod";
import { useForm } from "react-hook-form";
import { zodResolver } from "@hookform/resolvers/zod";
import type { FormValidationResult } from '@/lib/types';

const formSchema = z.object({
  name: z.string().min(2, "Name must be at least 2 characters"),
  email: z.string().email("Invalid email address"),
});

type FormData = z.infer<typeof formSchema>;

interface FormProps extends BaseComponentProps {
  onSubmit?: (data: FormData) => Promise<void>;
  onValidationChange?: (result: FormValidationResult) => void;
}
```

## Error Handling
- Use custom error types from [src/lib/errors.ts](mdc:src/lib/errors.ts)
- Use error handling hooks from [src/hooks/useErrorHandler.ts](mdc:src/hooks/useErrorHandler.ts)
- Use ErrorBoundary component for React error boundaries
- Handle async operations with proper error context

## Performance
- Use `"use client"` directive only when necessary
- Prefer server components when possible
- Use React.memo for expensive components
- Implement proper loading states